<!--Post: 3094--><!--Time: 1104448764-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3094"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tbear<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tbear<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, December 30, 2004 - 3:19 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I know this link has been used through out this site, but can somebody please look at these knots as shown. This particular style looks like a style my father used when I was growing up for tying things down. &#40;ie tying luggage to the roof of the car.&#41; My father was in both the Army and Navy and i remember him telling me he learned to tie like that in the military. &#40;and before it enters into anybody&#39;s mind, my father &#40;God rest his soul&#41; was nowhere near Kansas during this time period&#41; <BR> <BR><a href="http://ghostiesannex.myphotoalbum.com/view_photo.php?set_albumName=BTK&amp;id=knots" target=_top>http://ghostiesannex.myphotoalbum.com/view_photo.p hp?set_albumName=BTK&amp;id=knots</a> <BR> <BR>and to the author of this particular album, thank you!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3094-->
<!--Post: 3107--><!--Time: 1104458829-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3107"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, December 30, 2004 - 6:07 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I believe they are called Buntline Hitch knots.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3107-->
<!--Post: 3108--><!--Time: 1104460013-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3108"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Oliver<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Oliver<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, December 30, 2004 - 6:26 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Where do these clues come from on the link? Is this the FBI&#39;s way of releasing info?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3108-->
<!--Post: 3146--><!--Time: 1104511905-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3146"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tbear<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tbear<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, December 31, 2004 - 8:51 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I believe this is an individual that put this information together in this electronic &#39;photo album&#39;. The picture is from a newspaper article on the Otero murders, released before they knew it was a serial killer, I&#39;m sure. <BR> <BR>And thank you Johno, I&#39;ll do some digging. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3146-->
<!--Post: 3161--><!--Time: 1104533487-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3161"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bargle<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bargle<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, December 31, 2004 - 2:51 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Here&#39;s a link to an animated drawing of a Buntline hitch. It at least looks like a possible to me. The original photo is poor &#40;not the fault of the person  hosting the picture&#41; and it&#39;s difficult to be sure.  <BR><a href="http://www.dirauxwest.org/knots/buntline.htm" target=_top>http://www.dirauxwest.org/knots/buntline.htm</a><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3161-->
<!--Post: 3310--><!--Time: 1104777967-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3310"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Chiefron<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Chiefron<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, January 03, 2005 - 10:46 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Without being able to see all of the bitter ends it is hard to tell,but my Navy opinion would be that the knots on the right are just half hitches with different bitter ends or perhaps a cow hitch. The knots in the middle are too blurred to tell but if the two bitter ends showing on the left most were once together it may have been a bowline. The knot at the top of the photo appears to be a type of hitch again, possibly a single hitch.  <BR> <BR>It really does not take any special training to come up with these knots though. My father was in the Navy as well so the knots I learned as a child may have been influenced by that, but I &#34;learned&#34; several bowline variations through experimentation. <BR> <BR>Add to that, that military service, even naval service does not mean you learned knots. During basic training you learn a few, learn to recognize them by sight, but the vast majority of that knowledge fades very quickly unless you are assigned to a rate that uses them regularly. Unless BTK were a deck seaman or boatswains mate or signalman, it is unlikely he would have much knot tying skill. But as I mentioned, the knots in that picture are not very difficult and one could even bungle their way into tying the most complicated of them at center. The hitch knots are simple loops around an object or other line &#40;really that all any knot is&#41; that you learn as a child when tying your shoes for the first time.  <BR> <BR>Anyone growing up on a farm or a rural setting would learn the hitch knots and probably some variation of the bowline.  <BR> <BR>Ron<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3310-->
<!--Post: 3427--><!--Time: 1104889181-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3427"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Secret_squirrel<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Secret_squirrel<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 04, 2005 - 5:39 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Probably a long shot this late in the game but does anyone know of another perhaps better quality picture of the knots?  <BR> <BR>These are definitely a hitch of some sort and Im not familiar with the buntline hitch.  Ive tied the knots as best I can from the picture and Im leaning toward a munter hitch which may have been slightly modified.  Im a little more familiar with rescue knots rather than a wide variety of hitches so Im using some limited knowledge.  The one in the center/bottom of the photo looks like a simple overhand knot while the others as I said a is possibly a munter hitch with a personal modification.  Unfortunately, the clarity of the picture suggests some speculation and I can&#39;t tell what is tied to what. <BR> <BR>Im going to try and track down my local rope expert and get his opinion.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3427-->
<!--Post: 3511--><!--Time: 1104972121-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3511"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tbear<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tbear<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, January 05, 2005 - 4:42 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->here&#39;s my line of thought. on the news page if you scroll down to a listing on  sept 3 2004 there is a statement by a retired employee of WPD.If you you scroll down to the bottom of his statement there is a line about reports of a man in a tan uniform in the vicinity of the crime scenes. I know military uniforms have changes over the years but.....<!--/Text--></td></tr>
